  "body": "> Replaces #36, which GitHub auto-closed when #35 merged and its base branch was deleted. A PR whose base branch no longer exists cannot be reopened or retargeted, so this is the same commit rebased onto `main`. No work lost, no content changed.\n\n## What this adds\n\n**Detection.** The card enumerates every user-level `settings.json` that could apply on this machine, most likely first, and reports for each whether it exists and whether it already points at this proxy.\n\nUnder **WSL that is two genuinely different files** — your Linux home and your Windows home — which different Claude Code installs read. That is the failure mode most likely to waste an afternoon: configure one, launch the other, conclude the proxy is broken. Both are now listed and selectable.\n\n**Precedence warnings.** Enterprise `managed-settings.json` and its drop-in fragments are located per platform and surfaced as warnings, because they outrank the file being configured:\n\n| OS | managed path |\n| --- | --- |\n| macOS | `/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json` |\n| Linux / WSL | `/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json` |\n| Windows | `C:\\Program Files\\ClaudeCode\\managed-settings.json` |\n\nDrop-in `*.json` fragments in the sibling `managed-settings.d/` directory are included. Windows registry policies are out of scope and not read.\n\n## A warning we shipped that could never fire\n\n4.26.0 warned about a sibling `~/.claude/settings.local.json`. **Claude Code does not read that file.** Measured against 2.1.223 in a controlled home directory with identical content in each:\n\n| file in `~/.claude/` | requests reaching the proxy |\n| --- | --- |\n| `settings.json` | **4** |\n| `settings.local.json` | **0** |\n\nThe `settings.json` row is the positive control — it proves the method works, so the zero means something. `local` scope is repository-root only, exactly as the docs say; our code assumed a sibling of the user file.\n\nThe check is **deleted rather than extended**, `claude_local_settings_path()` goes with it as dead code, and the two tests that asserted the old behaviour now assert the measured one with the evidence recorded in a comment.\n\nProject-level files *do* outrank the user file, but the server cannot know which repository you are in — so that is a one-line static caveat in the card, explicitly labelled as not a detection result, rather than a filesystem scan.\n\n## API change\n\n`ClaudeSettingsStatus.local_override` → `overrides`, a list of `{path, scope, variables}` carrying only variable **names**, so an override file's secrets cannot leak through it. `GET /admin/api/claude-settings` returns `targets` in place of `suggested_paths`. No compatibility shim — every consumer updated.\n\n## Verification\n\nPer-platform detection, probed by monkeypatching `sys.platform` and the path constants:\n\n```\nmacOS    -> ['managed-settings.json', 'a.json']     drop-in *.json included\nlinux    -> ['managed-settings.json', 'a.json']     .txt ignored\nwindows  -> ['managed-settings.json', 'a.json']\nmalformed managed file -> no override, state unchanged, no exception\nthis machine (win32)   -> 1 candidate, 0 managed\n```\n\nLive against a 4.28.0 server:\n\n```json\n\"targets\": [{\"path\": \"C:\\Users\\...\\.claude\\settings.json\",\n             \"exists\": true, \"state\": \"unset\", \"is_default\": true}]\n\"overrides\": []\n```\n\n`suggested_paths` is gone from the codebase entirely; `/admin` and both assets serve 200.\n\n**Full suite after the rebase: 3182 passed, 14 failed** — the documented pre-existing baseline, unchanged on unmodified `main`.\n\n> Note for anyone running this locally: an `ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS` value in your shell environment defeats `Settings()` isolation and inflates that count to ~43. Unmodified `main` fails identically with it set. Run with `env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS` to see the true baseline. CI has no such variable.\n\n**One flake, fixed:** a new API test failed once in a full run while passing in isolation. It monkeypatched `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` and so depended on whether settings had already been cached in that xdist worker. The dependency is removed; the affected selection then ran clean three times.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Not visually verified.** The Chrome DevTools MCP server was disconnected during this work, so unlike 4.26.0 the rendered target list and precedence warnings were confirmed at the data layer only — element IDs present in the served HTML and wired in `admin.js`, `node --check` clean, but no screenshot.\n- Managed-path detection is exercised through monkeypatched constants, never against a real enterprise install.\n- `scope` remains a string with only `\"managed\"` in use, so project scope can be added later without another shape change.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
