    "message": "feat(admin): detect every Claude settings file and warn when one outranks it\n\nThe settings card configured a single path and warned about a sibling\nsettings.local.json. Both were too narrow, and the second was wrong.\n\nDetection now enumerates the user-level settings.json files that could apply on\nthis machine, most likely first, and reports for each whether it exists and\nwhether it already points at this proxy. Under WSL that is two genuinely\ndifferent files -- the Linux home and the Windows home -- which different Claude\nCode installs read, and which is the failure mode most likely to waste an\nafternoon here: configure one, launch the other, conclude the proxy is broken.\nEnterprise managed-settings.json and its drop-in fragments are located per\nplatform (macOS, Linux/WSL, Windows) and surfaced as precedence warnings, since\nthey outrank the file being configured.\n\nThe sibling settings.local.json check is deleted, not extended. Measured against\nClaude Code 2.1.223 in a controlled home directory with identical content:\n~/.claude/settings.json routed four requests to the proxy, ~/.claude/\nsettings.local.json routed none. That scope is repository-root only, so the\nwarning shipped in 4.26.0 could never be true. claude_local_settings_path() goes\nwith it as dead code, and the two tests that asserted the old behaviour now\nassert the measured one with the evidence recorded alongside.\n\nProject-level files do outrank the user file, but the server has no way to know\nwhich repository the user is in, so the card states that as a one-line caveat\ninstead of scanning the filesystem for it.\n\nClaudeSettingsStatus.local_override is replaced by overrides: a list of\n{path, scope, variables} carrying only variable NAMES, so an override file's\nsecrets cannot leak through it. GET /admin/api/claude-settings returns targets\nin place of suggested_paths.\n\nVerification:\n- Per-platform detection probed by monkeypatching sys.platform and the path\n  constants: each OS selects its own managed path, drop-in *.json files are\n  included and non-JSON ignored, a malformed managed file produces no override\n  and does not change state, and nothing raises.\n- Live against a 4.28.0 server: targets carries the default marked, overrides is\n  empty on a machine with no enterprise install, suggested_paths is gone from\n  the codebase entirely, and the admin page plus both assets serve 200.\n- Full suite: 3182 passed, 14 failed -- the documented pre-existing baseline\n  (8 defeated by real API keys in the shell environment, 6 PowerShell installer\n  tests), unchanged on unmodified main.\n- One new API test flaked once in a full run while passing in isolation and in\n  tests/api; it depended on monkeypatching ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN and therefore on\n  whether settings had already been cached in that xdist worker. The dependency\n  is removed and the affected selection ran clean three times.\n\nNot verified: the card was checked at the data layer only. The Chrome DevTools\nMCP server disconnected during this work, so unlike 4.26.0 there is no visual\nconfirmation of the rendered target list and precedence warnings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
